    "case_id": "47",
    "case_summary": "A 77-year-old woman reported a lump in her left breast that had been present for one month. She had a history of heart disease and had been receiving treatment for the past four years. Clinical examination revealed a firm, mobile, non-tender lump measuring 3 × 2 cm in the outer quadrant of her left breast. The skin, nipple, and areola of the affected breast appeared normal, and there was no significant lymphadenopathy in the axillary or cervical regions. The right breast was unremarkable. Mammography showed a 2 × 2 cm ill-defined mass with irregular margins in the left upper outer quadrant, without micro-calcifications. The right breast was normal. The lumpectomy specimen displayed a firm, grey-white, fibrous, un-encapsulated nodular tumor measuring 2 × 1.2 × 0.8 cm with infiltrative borders and no calcification. The closest margin was 0.5 cm from the tumor at the base. Frozen sections showed a tumor with predominant spindle cells exhibiting mild atypia within a sclerotic stroma, infiltrating the adjacent fat. A sentinel lymph node biopsy and/or axillary node dissection were not performed during surgery.\n\nHistological sections revealed a spindle cell tumor showing an infiltrative growth pattern with prominent areas of sclerosis reminiscent of keloid formation. The cells were mainly arranged in fascicles and displayed tapering nuclei with mild anisonucleosis. Mitoses were inconspicuous. Occasionally, the cells were plump with epithelioid shapes and revealed mild atypia with an occasional small cluster formation. Interspersed were foci of benign ductal hyperplasia and papillary hyperplasia, including a micropapilloma along with focal aggregates of chronic inflammatory cells. The micropapilloma did not show any significant atypia. No discrete squamous differentiation was identified. No focus of Ductal-carcinoma-in-situ (DCIS) was seen in any of the sections. The tumor cells were simultaneously diffusely positive for epithelial markers i.e. the various cytokeratins CK, CK7, High molecular weight (HMWCK) and epithelial membrane antigen (EMA), along with a mesenchymal marker i.e. vimentin. All the cytokeratins were positive in the interspersed benign ducts that acted as internal controls. The tumor cells were negative for Gross cystic disease fluid protein (GCDFP), estrogen (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR). The myoepithelial markers i.e. smooth muscle actin (SMA) and p63 showed focal, positive expression. S100 and Desmin were negative. Ki-67 (proliferation marker) showed focal positivity in less than 5% tumor cells. The tumor cells were negative for CD34 and CerbB-2/HER-2/neu. Ultrastructurally, the tumor cells embedded in a collagenous stroma showed fibroblastic and myoepithelial features along with presence of peripheral villous processes with a focal basal lamina and intercellular junctions.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
